From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000006 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 17:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] fix: don't let Steam's own window outrank a
 native-Wayland title

An Android title run through Valve's Lepton compat tool renders correctly and is
never shown: the guest's composer maps an `xdg_toplevel` and drives a healthy
commit loop, `gamescopectl screenshot` shows Steam, and the guest's own
`screencap` shows the title. The panel keeps Steam's Big Picture window for the
whole session.

The window is not missing an appid, and it is not missing from the focus
candidates. Instrumented on HW (`0006-focus-candidate-instrumentation`), the
global pass sees both windows and picks the wrong one:

    cand: global pick from 2 candidate(s), controlled=1(strategy=SteamControlled[1]
          ctrlwin=0x0 ctrlappids=3[413091,769,4545850])
    cand:   focus 0x1c00015 "Steam Big Picture Mode" appID=769 ... -> PRESENTING
    (the other candidate: xdg 0x1 appID=4545850, the title)

`pick_primary_focus_and_override()` walks `ctxFocusControlAppIDs` --
`GAMESCOPECTRL_BASELAYER_APPID`, published by Steam -- and takes the first
published appid that any candidate window carries. 769 is Steam's own client and
it is listed ahead of the title's 4545850, so Steam's window matches first and
the title never gets the screen. This is not a race. The same capture shows the
list arriving in the right order at launch, `[413091,4545850,769]`, and Steam
then demoting the title to the back: `[413091,769,4545850]`, where it stays.

Steam demotes it because gamescope never tells Steam the title has a window.
`determine_and_apply_focus()` builds both `GAMESCOPE_FOCUSABLE_APPS` and
`GAMESCOPE_FOCUSABLE_WINDOWS` from a loop that opens with

    if ( focusable_window->type != steamcompmgr_win_type_t::XWAYLAND )
        continue;

so a native-Wayland window is structurally invisible to the client whose ordering
gamescope then obeys. Steam waits for a focusable window that can never be
reported, concludes the app has no window, and puts its own UI back on top. The
ordering is therefore not Steam expressing a preference over the title -- it is
Steam having never heard of it.

So stop treating a Steam-client match as final. Hold it, keep reading the
published list, and let a native-Wayland (XDG) window matching a later appid take
it instead. Everything else is unchanged, deliberately:

  * Only Steam's own window is ever deferred (`window_is_steam()`: 769 or
    `isSteamLegacyBigPicture`). A game window matching an earlier appid still
    wins outright, exactly as before.
  * Only an XDG window can beat a deferred Steam window -- precisely the class
    that is missing from the focusable-apps report, so the class whose position
    in Steam's list carries no information. An XWayland window matching a later
    appid restores the deferred Steam window instead, which is the pick upstream
    would have made.
  * With no XDG candidate the deferred window is taken one iteration later, with
    the same `localGameFocused` upstream sets. An XWayland-only session is
    bit-identical.
  * Both local passes are bit-identical too, and structurally so: an
    `xwayland_ctx_t` candidate list contains no XDG windows, and the XDG list
    contains no Steam window. Only the global pass mixes the two, which is where
    the defect lives.

Known trade-off, accepted: while a native-Wayland title holds a published appid,
Steam cannot take the base layer back by reordering its list. The QuickAccess and
Steam overlays are unaffected -- those are `isOverlay` windows picked elsewhere
in `determine_and_apply_focus()`, not base-layer focus -- but "return to the
library while the title keeps running" will keep showing the title. The real fix
is to report XDG windows in `GAMESCOPE_FOCUSABLE_APPS` so Steam can order them
itself; that needs Steam-side behaviour we have not observed (the companion
`GAMESCOPE_FOCUSABLE_WINDOWS` triplet wants an X window id an XDG window does not
have), so it is not attempted here.

Not sent upstream, and nothing upstream addresses it: checked `origin/master` at
`eeaa4a52`, 41 commits past 3.16.25, which touches focus repeatedly (`focus_info`,
keyboard-focus recovery, override painting) but never the control-appid order and
never reports native-Wayland windows to Steam.

NOT YET VALIDATED ON HARDWARE. The mechanism and both list orderings above are
measured on a Pocket ACE; that the title presents once it wins focus is not. The
same capture shows the guest's window `HELD, no done commit` at the moment it was
picked locally, so a stuck commit queue is a separate live suspect and this patch
is necessary rather than known-sufficient. Rebuild with
`0006-focus-candidate-instrumentation` in `source.pin` to confirm the winner.
---
 src/steamcompmgr.cpp | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -3552,18 +3552,53 @@
 				}
 			}

+			// novadeck: Steam's own window does not get to outrank a native-Wayland title. Hold it
+			// and keep reading the list instead of taking it, because the order of that list cannot
+			// be trusted for a window Steam was never told about -- GAMESCOPE_FOCUSABLE_APPS is
+			// built from XWayland windows only, so Steam sees no window for the title's appid and
+			// demotes it below its own UI. Measured: [413091,4545850,769] at launch, then
+			// [413091,769,4545850] for the rest of the session.
+			steamcompmgr_win_t *pDeferredSteamFocus = nullptr;
+
 			for ( auto focusable_appid : ctxFocusControlAppIDs )
 			{
 				for ( steamcompmgr_win_t *focusable_window : vecPossibleFocusWindows )
 				{
 					if ( focusable_window->appID == focusable_appid )
 					{
+						// Hold the first one and move on to the next published appid; every other
+						// window matching THIS appid is a Steam window too.
+						if ( window_is_steam( focusable_window ) )
+						{
+							if ( !pDeferredSteamFocus )
+								pDeferredSteamFocus = focusable_window;
+							break;
+						}
+
+						// Only a native-Wayland window gets to beat what we are holding: it is the
+						// one class Steam cannot see and so cannot have ordered. For anything else
+						// upstream's answer was the Steam window it matched first -- give it back.
+						if ( pDeferredSteamFocus && focusable_window->type != steamcompmgr_win_type_t::XDG )
+						{
+							focus = pDeferredSteamFocus;
+							localGameFocused = true;
+							goto found;
+						}
+
 						focus = focusable_window;
 						localGameFocused = true;
 						goto found;
 					}
 				}
 			}
+
+			// Nothing beat it, so this is upstream's pick, one iteration later.
+			if ( pDeferredSteamFocus )
+			{
+				focus = pDeferredSteamFocus;
+				localGameFocused = true;
+				goto found;
+			}
 		}
 		else
 		{
--
